WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.760
The endgame of the Fast &amp; Furious franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:06.172
is coming. Can Dom and his&nbsp;
family survive? Let's ride.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:11.220
Almost as soon as "F9: The Fast&nbsp;
Saga" unspooled in theaters in 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:15.600
fans wanted to know "What's next?" And more&nbsp;
importantly, they wanted to know when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.920
follow-up and tenth installment in the "Fast&nbsp;
and Furious" franchise would arrive in theaters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.920
Entertainment Weekly reported in August 2021&nbsp;
that the still tentatively titled "Fast &amp; Furious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:32.580
10" would start filming in January 2022 in&nbsp;
advance of a release date of April 7, 2023.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:37.980
A few months later, Universal changed course&nbsp;
and pushed back the release of the film, now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:44.217
officially known as "Fast X". The film's release&nbsp;
date was officially changed to May 19, 2023.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.960
According to the plot summary on the film's&nbsp;
official website, "Fast X" is the beginning of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.760
the endgame for the franchise. Dominic Toretto and&nbsp;
his family stare down what's perhaps their most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:59.040
dangerous endeavor yet. Back in "Fast Five," Dom&nbsp;
and company took down drug kingpin Hernan Reyes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.380
Twelve years have passed, and his revenge-minded&nbsp;
son who witnessed his father's end has spent the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:09.540
entire period devising an elaborate and probably&nbsp;
fatal revenge plot. Surviving and fighting back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:14.520
will take the huge and ever-expanding crew around&nbsp;
the world, from Rome to Los Angeles to Portugal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:20.340
and even Antarctica. As this movie is supposed to&nbsp;
start to wrap up the series, plenty of long unseen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:25.020
characters — heroic and villainous — are likely&nbsp;
to return to take sides in the developing war.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:28.842
And it seems that not every character&nbsp;
will make it out of "Fast X" alive.

00:01:28.842 --> 00:01:34.182
"One of us might not come back&nbsp;
from this. But we have to fight."

00:01:34.182 --> 00:01:36.240
Director Louis Leterrier told Empire,

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.760
"There's a war brewing. It's coming to an end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.780
and it is a race to the finish. There&nbsp;
will be some tremendous casualties."

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:46.920
He also added that the movie promises to&nbsp;
be so exciting that it's suggested in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:51.840
title. He pronounces "Fast X" as "fast&nbsp;
ten" — like "fasten your seatbelts."

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:57.060
"Family" is extremely and repeatedly important&nbsp;
to the "Fast and Furious" franchise, so expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:03.240
plenty of familiar faces to return to the series&nbsp;
when it arrives in theaters in May 2023. Among the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:08.580
returning cast members are Vin Diesel as Dominic&nbsp;
Toretto, Jordana Brewster as Mia Toretto, Tyrese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:14.580
Gibson as Roman, Michelle Rodriguez as Letty&nbsp;
Ortiz, Sung Kang as Han, Charlize Theon as Cipher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:19.920
Helen Mirren as Queenie, John Cena as Jakob,&nbsp;
Nathalie Emmanuel as Ramsey, Chris "Ludacris"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:25.380
Bridges as Tej, Michael Rooker as Buddy, and&nbsp;
Jason Statham as villain-turned-hero Deckard Shaw.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:30.300
New additions to that already ridiculously&nbsp;
packed cast include "Reacher" star Alan Ritchson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:35.220
Oscar winners Brie Larson and Rita Moreno,&nbsp;
Portuguese film star Daniela Melchior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:40.080
and beloved action hero and "Aquaman"&nbsp;
himself, Jason Momoa, as Dante Reyes.

00:02:40.637 --> 00:02:45.060
The tenth official entry in the "Fast and&nbsp;
Furious" saga will bear the creative thumbprint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:51.060
of a director new to the franchise. Justin Lin,&nbsp;
who helmed 2009's "Fast and Furious," 2011's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:58.140
"Fast Five," 2013's "Fast &amp; Furious 6," and 2021's&nbsp;
"F9: The Fast Saga," left the directorial duties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.880
this time to Louis Letterier. Experienced&nbsp;
in the eye-popping action movie milieu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.560
Leterrier directed "Fast X" costar Jason&nbsp;
Statham in "The Transporter" and its sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:13.440
as well as the 2008 MCU entry "The Incredible&nbsp;
Hulk," the 2010 remake of "Clash of the Titans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.760
and all 10 episodes of Netflix's "The&nbsp;
Dark Crystal: Age of Resistance."

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.800
Lin didn't entirely leave the "Fast and Furious"&nbsp;
family, however. He collaborated on the "Fast X"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.880
screenplay with Daniel Casey, a re-pairing&nbsp;
of the team who wrote the script for "F9:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.440
The Fast Saga." That film's story came&nbsp;
from the mind of writer Alfredo Botello,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.893
and he similarly contributed&nbsp;
the plot outline for "Fast X."

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:40.200
After 10 mainline films, the franchise is&nbsp;
coming to a close. But as the trailers for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:45.000
"Fast X" suggest, the latest outing is only&nbsp;
the beginning of the end. "Fast X" may mark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.380
the end of "Fast &amp; Furious" as we know it, but&nbsp;
it's only the first part of the story. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:53.640
previous reports that "Fast X" would conclude&nbsp;
the series, Variety announced that "The Fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:58.380
Saga" would end with a two-part finale, one that&nbsp;
will reclaim the franchise's street racing roots.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:02.940
Though we don't yet know the title or the release&nbsp;
date for the final installment — which will mark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:07.980
Vin Diesel's 10th portrayal of Dom Toretto after&nbsp;
skipping out on "2 Fast 2 Furious" — we do know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:12.480
that the "Fast &amp; Furious" team is working hard&nbsp;
to develop a rewarding conclusion. When speaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.200
with "Variety," Diesel further explained&nbsp;
the decision to end the franchise with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:20.580
the eleventh feature by comparing the series&nbsp;
to J.R.R. Tolkien's "The Lord of the Rings."

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:22.620
"There's a reason Tolkien stopped writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.500
after a while. Because it's so&nbsp;
hard to continue mythologies."

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:29.760
The longer this franchise — which went to&nbsp;
outer space in "F9" — goes on, the harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:34.640
it is to keep it grounded and interesting.&nbsp;
Here's hoping "F11" finishes in first.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:39.720
Like many of the more recent "Fast &amp; Furious"&nbsp;
pictures, the cast and crew of "Fast X" find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:44.220
themselves traveling all over the world. While&nbsp;
the Torretto clan is based in Los Angeles, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:48.660
seem to find themselves breaking international&nbsp;
laws consistently. "Fast X" is no different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:53.160
This time, the street-racing spy thieves find&nbsp;
themselves filming in the United Kingdom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:57.660
Italy, and Portugal. Specifically, the&nbsp;
"Fast X" team made their way to Rome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:00.960
where actor Jason Momoa was seen&nbsp;
filming scenes for the movie.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.880
Aside from their international escapades, the&nbsp;
"Fast X" team returned to Angelino Heights to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.840
film the majority of the LA scenes,&nbsp;
with plenty of car chases and intense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:14.160
stunts making waves across their usual&nbsp;
stomping grounds. As "Variety" reported,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:19.500
locals weren't too enthusiastic about the film's&nbsp;
return, with some even organizing protests against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:24.540
the production. Ever since the first film was&nbsp;
released in 2001, popular "Fast &amp; Furious"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:28.920
filming locations — namely Bob's Market from&nbsp;
the first film — have been desired points of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:34.020
interest for fans and reckless drivers alike,&nbsp;
leading to dangerous incidents on local roads.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:37.800
Of course, not everyone was upset about the&nbsp;
movie being filmed in their neighborhood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:42.578
Homeowner Planaria Price made it clear that she&nbsp;
didn't want filming to end, telling Variety,

00:05:42.578 --> 00:05:46.003
"It's one of the most important&nbsp;
economic things we have in Los Angeles."

00:05:46.003 --> 00:05:51.360
Known for its clever retcons and devil-may-care&nbsp;
attitude, "The Fast Saga" isn't the easiest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:55.560
franchise to sit down and watch without the proper&nbsp;
context. Some of the earliest films actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.560 --> 00:06:00.840
take place later on in the timeline, while later&nbsp;
movies serve as prequels of sorts. Still, the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:05.640
first film, "The Fast and the Furious," is where&nbsp;
everyone should start, as it introduces the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:11.100
and the primary characters, namely Vin Diesel's&nbsp;
Dom Toretto and Paul Walker's Brian O'Conner.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:16.440
After the original 2001 feature, Brian's&nbsp;
adventures continue in the sequel "2 Fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:22.020
2 Furious," although Dom doesn't reunite with&nbsp;
his friend until 2009's "Fast &amp; Furious," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:26.220
is technically the fourth film but serves&nbsp;
as the third chronologically. From there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:31.020
"Fast Five" and "Fast &amp; Furious 6" continue the&nbsp;
plot threads established in the fourth film. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:35.580
third film released in the series, "The Fast and&nbsp;
the Furious: Tokyo Drift," takes place immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:40.680
after the sixth film leading into "Furious 7,"&nbsp;
which ties every previous installment together.

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:45.840
While "Furious 7" was notable as Walker's last&nbsp;
portrayal of Brian O'Conner before his untimely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:49.980
death, the series continued with "The Fate of&nbsp;
the Furious." Tying in with the main franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:53.700
"Fast &amp; Furious Presents: Hobbs &amp;&nbsp;
Shaw" takes place after "The Fate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:58.800
of the Furious" but before "F9." "Fast X"&nbsp;
will directly follow the events of "F9" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.347
begin the end of the Toretto clan's&nbsp;
international adventures together.

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:07.680
From the very first "Fast &amp; Furious" adventure,&nbsp;
Paul Walker's Brian O'Conner made his mark on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.880
franchise and quickly solidified himself&nbsp;
as a member of the Toretto family. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:15.060
becoming best friends with Dom and&nbsp;
having children with his sister Mia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:20.220
the Brian character was retired following&nbsp;
"Furious 7" due to Paul Walker's real-life death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:25.560
Both "The Fate of the Furious" and "F9" offered&nbsp;
explanations for his lack of involvement. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:28.980
the character remains a part of the&nbsp;
overarching "Fast &amp; Furious" story.

00:07:29.119 --> 00:07:32.479
"Brian and Mia got out of the&nbsp;
game when they became parents."

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:35.040
"We're not them."

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:39.180
It seems like "The Fast Saga" can only hold&nbsp;
out on showing Brian again for so long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:42.600
and it's becoming increasingly unlikely&nbsp;
that the character would continue to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:46.800
sit out each and every new conflict that&nbsp;
Dom, Letty, and the gang find themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:51.360
involved in. "Furious 7" sent off Brian&nbsp;
well in a respectful ending that honored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:55.320
Walker and the character. But with all&nbsp;
the constant teases and references,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:59.400
it's becoming increasingly less believable that&nbsp;
Brian wouldn't show up again at some point.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:03.060
When speaking with "Total Film," Vin&nbsp;
Diesel made it clear that "The Fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:06.960
Saga" won't end without a proper sendoff&nbsp;
for Paul Walker's breakout character.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.800
"I will give you this without spoiling&nbsp;
anything. I couldn't imagine this saga&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:13.500
ending without truly saying&nbsp;
goodbye to Brian O'Conner."

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:16.080
In almost every "Fast &amp; Furious" movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:19.860
we're introduced to a new villain who has&nbsp;
some strange hold over our favorite found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:23.820
family. This has ranged from international&nbsp;
terrorists to drug lords and assassins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:28.800
with some of Dom's most hated enemies becoming&nbsp;
uneasy allies later on. With "Fast X," Jason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:33.660
Momoa has been added to the ever-growing list&nbsp;
of adversaries, but like "Furious 7" nemesis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:38.820
Deckard Shaw before him, Momoa's Dante Reyes also&nbsp;
has a previous connection to Dom and his crew.

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:43.680
As revealed in the "Fast X" trailers, Dante's&nbsp;
time in "The Fast Saga" extends back to "Fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:48.780
Five," when Dom, Brian, and their crew took&nbsp;
down drug lord Hernan Reyes. While Momoa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:52.920
obviously didn't show up in "Fast Five,"&nbsp;
clever retconning and repurposed footage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:57.480
have included him in the narrative. Though it&nbsp;
was technically Luke Hobbs who executed Reyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:01.680
the makeshift Toretto family was ultimately&nbsp;
responsible for the drug lord's defeat.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:04.560
Hoping to enact his revenge&nbsp;
against his father's killers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:08.400
Momoa's character is rooted within the&nbsp;
franchise's mythology and aims to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:13.320
the perfect antithesis of Dom. As director&nbsp;
Louis Leterrier described him to Esquire,

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:18.360
"He's also the polar opposite of Dom. He's all&nbsp;
color and flamboyance. It's the [yin] and yang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:21.037
the Christ and the Anti-Christ — the anti-Dom."

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:25.620
One of the strangest additions to the later&nbsp;
years of "The Fast Saga" was the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:31.560
spymaster Mr. Nobody. Played to perfection by Kurt&nbsp;
Russell, Mr. Nobody first appeared in "Furious 7,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:36.840
at the tail end of Brian O'Conner's tenure in the&nbsp;
series. With the advent of this secret super-spy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:40.620
Dom and his family were ushered into&nbsp;
a new world of international espionage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:44.940
with potentially disastrous consequences.&nbsp;
Appearing in the last three installments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:49.680
Russell's character isn't slated to appear&nbsp;
in "Fast X," leaving his fate ambiguous.

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:53.580
At the beginning of "F9: The Fast&nbsp;
Saga," Mr. Nobody is credited with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:58.320
capturing Cipher and transporting her&nbsp;
by plane back to the US. Unsurprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:01.980
this transport doesn't go as planned&nbsp;
and Mr. Nobody's plane goes down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:07.080
leaving Cipher to join Jakob Toretto on his&nbsp;
revenge mission against Dom. In all the chaos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:11.580
the fate of Mr. Nobody is never officially&nbsp;
addressed. Though Han eventually explains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:15.600
how the agent helped him fake his death,&nbsp;
his current whereabouts remain unclear.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:18.720
Although Russell is not returning&nbsp;
for "Fast X" — at least as far as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:22.680
we know — Mr. Nobody's daughter Tess is&nbsp;
set to appear. Played by Brie Larson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:27.000
the trailers suggest that Tess will aid Dom and&nbsp;
his team in their mission against Dante Reyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:30.960
acting in a similar capacity as her&nbsp;
father did when working with the team.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:36.240
Ever since "Fast Five," Dwayne Johnson has been&nbsp;
an iconic part of the "Fast &amp; Furious" franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:41.100
though his involvement has not been without some&nbsp;
controversy. Series star Vin Diesel had initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:45.000
fought for Johnson's casting as government&nbsp;
bounty hunter Luke Hobbs in the fifth film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:49.080
but the two clashed on set. Johnson&nbsp;
reprised his role in "Fast &amp; Furious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:53.700
6" and "Furious 7," but took a break during&nbsp;
the eighth installment, instead developing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:57.979
his own spin-off with Jason Statham in&nbsp;
"Fast &amp; Furious Presents: Hobbs &amp; Shaw."

00:10:57.979 --> 00:11:00.439
"When it comes to the fate of the&nbsp;
world, it becomes my business."

00:11:00.439 --> 00:11:06.060
Johnson also skipped "F9: The Fast Saga," though&nbsp;
his spin-off co-star reprised his role as Shaw for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:10.320
an uncredited after-credits sequence. While&nbsp;
Statham is set to return as Shaw once more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:14.940
for "Fast X," Hobbs will not be joining him.&nbsp;
Since Johnson's character was so important to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:19.500
"Fast Five" — he executed Hernan Reyes, after&nbsp;
all — it will be interesting to see how "Fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:24.600
X" deals with Hobbs' absence and if Dante Reyes&nbsp;
will even attempt to assassinate Hobbs as well.

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:29.040
While Dwayne Johnson has repeatedly distanced&nbsp;
himself from "The Fast Saga," fans still hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:33.180
to see Luke Hobbs once more before the&nbsp;
franchise closes out. With no concrete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:37.260
news on a potential "Hobbs &amp; Shaw" sequel&nbsp;
and Johnson's disinterest in returning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:40.680
to the main series, it's probably a&nbsp;
safe bet to assume that Hobbs' story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:45.000
will come to a close off-screen. It's&nbsp;
not ideal, but that's show business.

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:50.340
"Fast &amp; Furious" fans are also eager to see&nbsp;
Gal Gadot's Gisele return. First appearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:54.900
in the fourth installment, "Fast &amp; Furious,"&nbsp;
Gisele joined Dom's crew as a weapons expert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:59.400
and specialist. As a former CIA agent,&nbsp;
she was an enormous asset to the team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:04.440
in both "Fast Five" and "Fast &amp; Furious&nbsp;
6." Sadly, Gisele met her end saving her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:08.820
boyfriend Han near the tail end of the sixth&nbsp;
movie, and many fans still aren't over it.

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:10.620
"Gisele."

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:19.560
Aside from some passing mentions and a&nbsp;
deleted scene from "Furious 7," Gadot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:23.640
has been too busy playing Wonder Woman&nbsp;
in the DCEU to return full-time to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:28.440
franchise. After Han's death in "Tokyo Drift"&nbsp;
and subsequent return in "F9" — which followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:32.340
Letty's "death" and resurrection a few&nbsp;
films prior — it's as possible as ever that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:36.780
Gisele's demise could've been faked also,&nbsp;
especially given her ties to Mr. Nobody.

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:40.320
While there hasn't been any official&nbsp;
confirmation regarding Gisele's return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:44.400
to "The Fast Saga," "The Direct" reported&nbsp;
that Gal Gadot had filmed scenes for "Fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:49.440
X." In response to questions regarding Gisele's&nbsp;
possible resurrection, Han actor Sung Kang told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:53.760
"Digital Spy" that there was no reason the two&nbsp;
couldn't be reunited by the franchise's end.

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:56.880
"Why can't she? ... Who doesn't want&nbsp;
a load of Gal Gadot in their life?"

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:58.800
If she does return, odds are good that her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:01.200
survival will be revealed&nbsp;
in a post-credits sequence.

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:05.040
On March 10, with just over two months&nbsp;
until the film was set to hit theaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:10.020
Vin Diesel filmed a short scene characterized&nbsp;
as a "tag" or "button," or what fans might just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:13.680
call a "post-credits scene." He may have&nbsp;
filmed it with a co-star whose identity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:18.300
wasn't confirmed or revealed. Could Gal&nbsp;
Gadot be his alleged secret scene partner?

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:22.800
It seems that with almost every new venture&nbsp;
into "The Fast Saga," the movies get bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:27.240
and increasingly more expensive. Following the&nbsp;
success of the fifth and sixth films, "Furious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:33.420
7" was given a larger budget that, after the death&nbsp;
of Paul Walker, quickly grew to $250 million for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:38.280
digital recreations and reshoots. The following&nbsp;
film, "The Fate of the Furious," was granted a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:43.140
similar budget, while the next two franchise&nbsp;
installments, "Hobbs &amp; Shaw" and "F9," cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:48.840
$200 million each. These budgets were a far cry&nbsp;
from the original film's budget of $38 million.

00:13:49.404 --> 00:13:53.760
As the first part of a greater franchise finale,&nbsp;
"The Wrap" reported that the new film's budget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:59.640
is pushing $340 million, making it one of the&nbsp;
most expensive films ever made. In comparison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:05.580
"Avengers: Endgame" had a budget of $356 million&nbsp;
and the budget for James Cameron's "Avatar:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:09.600
The Way of Water" was reported to&nbsp;
be somewhere around $350 million.

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:14.220
Though "Fast X" is less expensive than those two,&nbsp;
it's still more costly than popular blockbusters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:18.840
including "Avengers: Infinity War" or "Pirates&nbsp;
of the Caribbean: At World's End." Exact numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:23.760
aside, the point is that "Fast X" will be huge,&nbsp;
and while a big budget does not necessarily equal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:28.200
a hit, we can hope that the extra cash will&nbsp;
be used wisely to bring the story to life.

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:32.460
If one thing is obvious about "The Fast&nbsp;
and the Furious" and its many sequels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.460 --> 00:14:36.000
it's that every new addition gets&nbsp;
just a bit bigger and even harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:40.260
to believe. A film that was originally&nbsp;
about illegal racing, stolen DVD players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:44.940
and one cop's infiltration of a street gang&nbsp;
quickly shifted genres and became something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.940 --> 00:14:49.320
bigger than anyone had previously thought&nbsp;
possible. In the sequel, Brian goes undercover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:53.640
to take down a drug kingpin. In "The Fast and&nbsp;
the Furious: Tokyo Drift," everything changes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:58.320
focusing instead on American Sean Boswell&nbsp;
in Tokyo as he falls into street racing.

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:02.340
From the fourth film on, the franchise&nbsp;
found its voice as an adrenaline-fueled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:06.888
car-centric heist series, but it evolved&nbsp;
into something even bigger from there, too.

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:11.078
"I don't have friends. I got family."

00:15:11.078 --> 00:15:15.180
The franchise since has turned into a&nbsp;
globe-trotting action series with ties to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:20.400
spy genre, with each film becoming increasingly&nbsp;
unrealistic as the series pressed onward. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:24.480
that's not what "Fast X" director Louis&nbsp;
Leterrier hopes to do with the 10th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:27.900
installment. The director told "Esquire"&nbsp;
when discussing his vision for the film,

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:32.400
"What I wanted to do on this one, because it's&nbsp;
very much my style, was to ground it more in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:38.460
reality. I wanted it to feel like 'Fast 1,' 'Fast&nbsp;
3,' and 'Fast 5' — movies that feel grounded."

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:42.840
Whether that actually translates to the big&nbsp;
screen remains to be seen, but audiences will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:47.520
get a chance to see for themselves when "Fast&nbsp;
X" comes roaring into theaters on May 19.

